ackson scoop jackson of  washington a  conservative democrat um carter gets  elected  and does what a lot of us i i was in a  group with admiral zumwalt jean  kirkpatrick  um people like that and and  we were obviously drifting away and  walter mondale was vice president  wanted to keep all of us in democratic  party  so in 1980 this would be about  may june he said all of you should come  in  and meet with president carter  and this would reaffirm your loyalty to  the democratic party so we all went in  to see the president  and mondale met with us first in the  cabinet room there are about a dozen of  us  and he gave a terrific pitch  and all of us were sort of thinking well  i don't know  you know we've been democrats and then  the president came in the room  and we had agreed that one of us would  be the spokesman and he said mr  president you give us a great honor to  meet with us  we want to support you and remember this  is um  soviets invaded afghanistan in 1979  and he said we want to support you and  while it's true that we  we were very critical of your foreign  policy  since the soviet invasion of afghanistan  we do think the  policy has changed and toughened up and  we're  hopeful that we'll boom president carter  interrupted him  and said i don't have two foreign  policies i have the same foreign policy  i had  from the beginning  so that was the end of our speeches and  uh the president stayed about 15 minutes  which was probably  long enough to tell him what he needed  to know about us and vice versa  and as we all left that meeting every  one of us  shook our heads and said to the others  reagan  that was the moment that did it  thank you for the speech i am a uh  former republican due to disillusionment  during the reagan years  as a kid i went door-to-door for ronald  reagan in a blue-collar neighborhood i  just about got killed by all the  democrats there  but the big disappointment of the reagan  years i'd like you to comment on  big disappointment for me were the three  appointments that he made to the supreme  court only scalia  stands as a fine choice it seems that  the o'connor appointment was squandered  and it seemed that the bork appointment  was fantastic  but the great communicator failed to  make the case  to the american people and to get them  on the court so i think  i think two out of three liberals is a  bad record and democrats don't make that  mistake there's not one hard  conservative on the court that  the democrats didn't know was a  conservative tilly until he got there so  if you could comment on that  well first i think your facts are right  that is that democrats have a better  record of  always appointing a liberal and  republicans do have always appointed a  conservative and there are other  examples  president ford appointed john paul  stevens  president george h.w bush appointed  david suter  so this happens repeatedly  and  one of the reasons why has got to be  the desire to get an appointment through  what was in those days a democratic  senate  so you choose someone who appears to be  moderate  i think that's a mistake but i think  that's one of the things that goes into  the minds of  the political guys in an administration  um in the case of suitor  and president the first president bush  he was sold a bill of goods i think  frankly by his chief of staff  john sununu who was from new hampshire  and said you like him he's a  conservative guy  that was just wrong um  with bork the tragedy is  he appointed scalia as i recall and then  bork and if it had been bork and then  scalia  the president was stronger earlier on  and i think he would have gotten the  bork  appointment it would have been a  brilliant justice  but i it may have been i mean it looks  as if it was impossible then  once the democrats frankly led by  senator kennedy made a decision to just  go all out to  to smear judge borg  president bush made this mistake or may  have made this mistake  too he certainly didn't in the people  who got on the court he may have made  the mistake with harriet myers we'll  never know  because she had no judicial record  whereas  john roberts and samuel alito both had  records that  from the bush point of view you could  look at and say i know exactly the kind  of judge he is  but there's a real lesson here and and  it's the lesson you're  pointing to um we if you believe in  strict construction  as all of the current republican  candidates say they do  um you cannot take a chance on this this  is a lifetime appointment you've got to  have i would say  a judge my own opinion you've got to  have somebody who's been  a judge so that there's an there's a  real track record  as a judge or you run the risk that that  you're going to get what unfortunately  president reagan got  thank you so much  you must be a senior  this is a centenary celebration  i would like your comments on  how you i mean we have seen over the  years the assessment of the historians  back and forth on the reagan years i  mean i know you  are totally supportive i'm totally  supportive  what do you see is the academic  atmosphere  presently about the reagan years  it's a lot better than it used to be  this happened with president eisenhower  too most academics if you go to  1960 were saying that eisenhower  resisted dope  it began about five years after that a  man  professor named fred greenstein wrote a  book called the hidden hand presidency  that said if you think eisenhower was a  dope you are a fool  and began the reassessment of  eisenhower's extremely deft presidency  so reagan had a little bit of that  benefit now the academic left of course  hated him  but it's coming around already and  you begin to see more and more um  books and articles about him that say  that are the kind of  well of course i didn't agree with him  but  i didn't like him but i didn't vote for  him but  there's an acknowledgment now of his  fantastic skills as a  communicator there's also i think much  more of an appreciation of his mind  because the the view of many on the left  then was well he's a great communicator  he was an actor but he hasn't there's  nothing in there  but now we've seen his diaries and we  know that for decades  he was writing down thoughts and  analyses and comments about  world affairs now people begin to  appreciate  um what he was like  i hope ultimately this benefits  president bush  too but i'll leave you with with with a  with a story  it's hard to for academics i think to  fully appreciate  reagan they appreciate him a lot more  now than they used to  but one of the reasons is because he  like many politicians  had a sense about people  that doesn't show up on paper but that  you need to have to be a great  leader story the  leading industrial countries in those  days the g7 met in venice for their  summit and it was a good summit  and we had the next one the following  year  and as reagan and schultz are flying  back from venice  reagan said to schultz well george  where next this turned out to be the  williamsburg summit in williamsburg  virginia  he said we're next and we're going to  have the best summit  of all and schultz thought well you know  pep talk right and he said yes sir mr  president  and reagan said no no no no george  during this summit i have been watching  the other  presidents and prime ministers  and i have been asking myself when are  they  pleased and when are they displeased  when do they think they have had a good  and useful session and when do they  go away annoyed and frustrated and the  answer  he said to schultz the answer is  they like the sessions where they get to  talk a lot  not a great shock no one had ever said  this before and he said george  you may not like this but here's what i  want you to organize the williamsburg  summit  i want a lot of sessions  of leaders only i want seven of us in  the room so that we can have a lot  of conversation i do not want sessions  where the prime ministers  and the finance ministers and the  foreign ministers  and so forth chiefs of staff are in the  room i don't want  meetings with 40 people in the room that  is how they organized the williamsburg  summit  and reagan was exactly right and all the  other leaders went away from that summit  saying  wow we've never had this good summit  we've never been able to communicate  with each other  and discuss the problems we face more  openly  more candidly reagan figured that out  because one of the things that he was  doing as  president in addition to  thinking about the problems that he had  to solve  was looking at the man or in the case of  margaret thatcher the woman  sitting at the other end of the sofa or  across the table who is this person  what makes this person tick what do i  need to do  to get this person to give me what i  want for the united states  it's an enormous talent it's the talent  of a great great  politician and i think it's going to be  a while before  all of the memoirs are fully digested  and academics realize  but we are i would say we're halfway  there and the picture of ronald reagan  that they were giving in 1989  is is already really pretty much gone  and uh  i'm grateful for that and i'm confident  that he will be regarded  for ending the cold war and for many  other things as one of our greatest  presidents thank you very much it's been  a pleasure to be with you tonight  you
 

